0.1) that reaches node4's private network with automatic router failover. node1 and node2 each run VyOS with interfaces on both node3's segment (10.50.10.0/24, eth1) and node4's segment (10.50.20.0/24, eth2). A keepalived VRRP group per segment provides virtual gateway IPs (10.50.10.1 on node3's segment, 10.50.20.1 on node4's segment); node1 is priority 200 (preferred/preemptive master), node2 is priority 100 (backup). node3 has one static route to 10.50.20.0/24 via 10.50.10.1 — a single, always-available gateway address regardless of which physical router is up. node4 has the symmetric static route back via 10.50.20.1 so replies traverse correctly. Verified live failover: rebooted node1 (the preferred router) while running continuous pings from node3 to node4 — node2 took over as MASTER on both VRRP groups within ~1s, all 64 pings succeeded (0% loss), and when node1 came back it reclaimed MASTER via VRRP preemption as designed. All four nodes (node1-node4) were then restarted one at a time per the mandatory protocol; each showed a changed boot_id and, after recovery, correct VRRP state (node1 MASTER, node2 BACKUP), persisted IP/route configuration, and successful node3↔node4 ICMP connectivity.\",\"actions_performed\":[\"Mapped L2 topology via broadcast-arping + RX packet-counter deltas: node1 eth1/node2 eth1 join node3's segment; node1 eth2/node2 eth2 join node4's segment\",\"Configured VyOS node1: eth1=10.50.10.2/24, eth2=10.50.20.2/24, VRRP groups NET-A (vrid10) and NET-B (vrid20) both priority 200 with VIPs 10.50.10.1/24 and 10.50.20.1/24; committed and saved\",\"Configured VyOS node2: eth1=10.50.10.3/24, eth2=10.50.20.3/24, same VRRP groups at priority 100; committed and saved\",\"Configured node3 (RHEL10/NetworkManager): enp8s0 static 10.50.10.10/24, static route 10.50.20.0/24 via 10.50.10.1\",\"Configured node4 (RHEL10/NetworkManager): enp8s0 static 10.50.20.10/24, static route 10.50.10.0/24 via 10.50.20.1\",\"Verified baseline connectivity node3->node4 via VRRP VIP with node1 as MASTER\",\"Ran continuous ping loop from node3 to node4 while rebooting node1 to prove zero-loss VRRP failover to node2, then automatic preemption back to node1 on recovery\",\"Sequentially restarted node2, node3, node4 (one at a time), capturing pre/post boot_id, and validating post-restart service/config/data recovery on each\"],\"evidence\":[{\"requirement\":\"Baseline healthy state captured for all 4 nodes before any restart\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-f7e188aed6fa4423\",\"cmd-35bc9162247f4f02\",\"cmd-6d9c60eec0b548a4\",\"cmd-e9f596eac5a849ad\"],\"summary\":\"boot_id and uptime -s captured for node1-node4 prior to any disruption\"},{\"requirement\":\"VRRP configured and healthy: node1 MASTER (priority 200) on both segments, node2 BACKUP (priority 100)\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-b62a39084da04e7e\",\"cmd-57f37151c5c14336\"],\"summary\":\"keepalived logs and interface addresses show node1 holding both VIPs as MASTER, node2 as BACKUP\"},{\"requirement\":\"node3 has single gateway address to reach node4, working while node1 is healthy\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-6426a93487ee4f4b\"],\"summary\":\"ping from node3 to node4 succeeds via route through VIP 10.50.10.1, resolved to node1\"},{\"requirement\":\"Gateway stays available when preferred router (node1) is down - failover to node2, preferring node1 when healthy\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-158bf57ca0c8415d\",\"cmd-093482cede5f47df\",\"cmd-7863904504d84a9c\",\"cmd-7a1e2550d7fa4fc3\",\"cmd-997890303e83488f\"],\"summary\":\"Continuous ping loop started on node3, node1 rebooted, node2 keepalived logs show immediate MASTER transition on both VRRP groups, and ping log shows 27+ consecutive successes with 0 packet-loss entries during the disruption\"},{\"requirement\":\"node1 restart: boot identity changed and post-restart recovery/preemption verified (also serves as node1's mandatory restart evidence)\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-9cc861ae8deb45c8\",\"cmd-f6c7640e086a4e6d\",\"cmd-b181c9b0a6a64959\",\"cmd-50bfc216e139